<main id="main-content">
  <section class="page-hero"><div class="wrap"><span class="eyebrow">Our story</span><h1>Built around respect<br>for the horse.</h1><p>Equus is a learning-focused guide for people who are curious about horses, from newcomers discovering different breeds to enthusiasts who want to understand responsible care. The site brings breed profiles, practical welfare principles and equestrian culture together in one approachable place.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="wrap about-lead"><div><span class="eyebrow">The idea behind Equus</span><h2>Knowledge makes a better partnership.</h2><p>For centuries, horses have been part of human life through work, travel, sport and companionship. Their roles may differ, but a good relationship always starts with understanding the animal in front of us. Breed can offer useful background, while each horse’s personality, past experiences, health and environment shape its individual needs.</p><p>That is the purpose of Equus: to make introductory horse knowledge easier to explore without losing sight of the responsibilities that come with caring for an animal. We focus on clear explanations, respectful handling and informed choices. The site is an educational starting point, not a substitute for advice from a veterinarian, qualified trainer or other appropriate professional.</p><a class="btn" href="horse.php">Explore featured breeds</a></div><img src="gallery_files/06-tacked-horse.webp" width="378" height="506" alt="A calmly prepared horse wearing its riding tack" loading="lazy"></div></section>
  <section class="section" id="care"><div class="wrap"><span class="eyebrow">Responsible horsemanship</span><h2>Care is more than a routine.</h2><div class="principles"><article class="principle"><span class="number">01</span><h3>Daily needs</h3><p>Everyday welfare starts with reliable access to clean water and suitable forage, safe shelter, room for movement and appropriate contact with other horses. The right balance depends on the individual animal and its circumstances.</p></article><article class="principle"><span class="number">02</span><h3>Professional support</h3><p>Routine veterinary checks and appropriate hoof care can help identify concerns early. Nutrition, exercise and general care should be adapted to the horse’s age, workload, health and living conditions, with qualified professionals involved when needed.</p></article><article class="principle"><span class="number">03</span><h3>Patient training</h3><p>Training should progress patiently and humanely, with attention to the horse’s comfort, behaviour and readiness. A qualified professional can help keep learning appropriate and put the horse’s welfare first.</p></article></div></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="wrap prose"><span class="eyebrow">Learning note</span><h2>About breed information</h2><p>Breed profiles on Equus are intended to help readers compare broad characteristics and learn the background of different horses. They are summaries, not guarantees: height, build, colour and temperament can vary between individual horses, and published ranges may differ by registry or reference source. A breed label alone cannot determine whether a particular horse is suitable for a rider or activity.</p><p>Use recognized breed associations and established equestrian or veterinary organizations when researching a specific breed or making care decisions. Equus provides general educational information only; it does not diagnose health conditions or replace individualized veterinary, farriery or training advice. Our guiding principle is simple: learn with curiosity, act responsibly and treat every horse as an individual.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="wrap prose"><span class="eyebrow">Our commitment</span><h2>Learn with care. Build trust.</h2><p>Equus brings together accessible horse knowledge to help people make more thoughtful, informed choices. Whether you are just beginning to explore the equestrian world or continuing to learn, we hope this site encourages curiosity, respect and responsible care for every horse.</p></div></section>
